    "narrative": "For July 22, 2026, the Ghost Ride the WHIP lineup is anchored by **Tyler Soderstrom** (76.91), who earns our top spot thanks to a premium matchup against the fading command of Kelly. With a +19% boost from the opposing SP skill and a favorable slot at #2, he represents our most efficient path to category production. He is closely followed by **James Wood** (71.14) and **Mickey Moniak** (69.64), both of whom benefit from massive park factors (+14%) that elevate their ceiling. **Vinnie Pasquantino** (67.14) also demands attention; despite a wind-in warning at the stadium, his +14% platoon edge against Roupp keeps his output well within the \"Strong\" start tier.\n\nOur most difficult sit-start decision involved **Josh Naylor** (59.86). While he sits just outside our \"Strong\" threshold, he was ultimately displaced by the higher efficiency scores of **Isaac Paredes** (65.34) and **Austin Riley** (64.43). Despite **Josh Naylor**\u2019s consistent base projection, a negative park factor and a documented struggle in his 28-PA sample against Singer made it difficult to justify a start over our higher-ceiling core. Meanwhile, we remain wary of the weather risks facing **Vinnie Pasquantino** and **Seiya Suzuki** due to the wind-in flags, though their high positional value outweighs the potential suppression of home run power today. \n\nWe are closely monitoring **Corbin Carroll** (59.77) as he nears the lower edge of our active threshold. While we value the elite sprint speed he brings to our stolen base hunt, the combination of a -6% platoon disadvantage and a -4% penalty for the specific opposing pitcher\u2019s ability to hold runners makes him a high-risk play. Finally, we caution that **Gabriel Moreno** (51.45) and **Dalton Rushing** (47.53) are operating in marginal territory; while both provide necessary stability behind the plate, their production is suppressed today by defensive-minded matchups and lower-order placement, keeping our expectations tempered for the catching duo.",
